+## Interpretation
+
+AUROC describes ranking of malignant versus benign records. Brier score, log loss and
+calibration plots describe probability quality; high AUROC does not establish reliable
+probabilities. At the training-selected operating points, FN means a malignant record
+classified negative, and FP means a benign record that might be sent for unnecessary
+escalated review. These are errors in this historical dataset, not observed clinical benefits.
+
+Compare leaf size and depth within each max_features setting using parameter_effects.csv
+and figures/inner_parameter_results.png. These fixed-grid patterns reflect joint model
+settings and overlapping training data. They do not isolate causal parameter effects.
+Training AUROC is optimistic because it evaluates the same observations used for fitting.
+The training–validation gap is a diagnostic, not a reason for post-hoc model changes.
+
+The feature plot summarizes splits selected in training models. Impurity importance has
+no effect direction, can favor variables with more available split points and can shift
+among correlated predictors. Radius, perimeter and area and their mean/worst summaries
+are related. Family-level totals describe where importance is allocated, but do not fix
+these biases. This analysis neither identifies causal effects nor demonstrates a
+particular interaction. No features were selected from these plots.
